      "title": {
        "segmentId": "m00252:title:sect-00001-title",
        "text": "Acid-Catalyzed Epoxide Opening"
      },
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "para",
          "id": "para-00001",
          "segmentId": "m00252:para:para-00001"
        },
        {
          "type": "figure",
          "id": "fig-00001",
          "class": "unnumbered",
          "caption": null,
          "media": {
            "alt": {
              "segmentId": "m00252:alt:fig-00001-alt",
              "text": "Two reactions are shown: 1,2-epoxycyclohexane becomes trans-1,2-cyclohexanediol (86 percent yield) via intermediates, and cyclohexene reacts with bromine to yield trans-1,2-dibromocyclohexane."
            },
            "src": "../../media/OChem_18_06_001.jpg",
            "mimeType": "image/jpeg"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "para",
          "id": "para-00002",
          "segmentId": "m00252:para:para-00002"
        },
        {
          "type": "figure",
          "id": "fig-00002",
          "class": "unnumbered scaled-down",
          "caption": null,
          "media": {
            "alt": {
              "segmentId": "m00252:alt:fig-00002-alt",
              "text": "An epoxide reacts with H X in the presence of ether to yield trans 2-halocyclohexanol. The X refers to any halogen atom."
            },
            "src": "../../media/OChem_18_06_002.jpg",
            "mimeType": "image/jpeg"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "para",
          "id": "para-00003",
          "segmentId": "m00252:para:para-00003"
        },
        {
          "type": "figure",
          "id": "fig-00003",
          "class": "unnumbered scaled-down",
          "caption": null,
          "media": {
            "alt": {
              "segmentId": "m00252:alt:fig-00003-alt",
              "text": "Two chemical reactions show the transformation of epoxy compounds with hydrochloric acid and ether, resulting in chloro-alcohol products with specific atom arrangements and varying chlorine positions."
            },
            "src": "../../media/OChem_18_06_003.jpg",
            "mimeType": "image/jpeg"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "para",
          "id": "para-00004",
          "segmentId": "m00252:para:para-00004"
        },
        {
          "type": "para",
          "id": "para-00005",
          "segmentId": "m00252:para:para-00005"
        },
        {
          "type": "figure",
          "id": "fig-00004",
          "class": "scaled-down",
          "caption": {
            "segmentId": "m00252:caption:fig-00004-caption"
          },
          "media": {
            "alt": {
              "segmentId": "m00252:alt:fig-00004-alt",
              "text": "The reaction of 1,2-epoxy-1-methylcyclohexane with hydrogen bromide forms a stable tertiary carbocation, producing 2-bromo-2-methylcyclohexanol. The secondary carbocation is not formed in the reaction."
            },
            "src": "../../media/OChem_18_06_004.jpg",
            "mimeType": "image/jpeg"
          }
        },
